  pm-doxj,related,"2026-07-25T17:59:11.780Z",null,null
  pm-xp1xsw,related,"2026-07-25T17:59:11.780Z",null,null
body: "## Rationale\n\nThe portfolio has six deadlined outcome milestones (pm-j82fd3, pm-9rgaal, pm-w7ccmv, pm-o87av6, pm-xp1xsw, pm-t4d7nz) and a promotion policy (pm-m08hza) that demands evidence-gated movement. Nothing computes whether those deadlines are achievable. Deadlines are asserted; they are never checked against the throughput the workspace has actually demonstrated.\n\npm-pee5 delivers descriptive flow metrics — cycle time, lead time, throughput, aging WIP — i.e. what already happened. This item is the predictive half: given recorded throughput, the open dependency graph, and per-item size signals, produce a calibrated distribution over completion dates and keep score on whether those forecasts came true.\n\nDeterministic single-point estimates (sum of `estimated_minutes`) are the anti-pattern this replaces: they ignore variance, dependency serialization, and the empirical fact that estimates are biased.\n\n## Method\n\n1. **Empirical throughput sampling.** Sample completed-items-per-period from immutable history (`close` events with real completion time — see pm-bwnclq for completion-vs-tracker-close separation). No distribution assumption; the workspace's own record is the distribution.\n2. **Monte Carlo over the dependency graph.** Draw throughput samples, walk the remaining scope in topological order over `blocked_by`/`parent` edges (pm-7ob5 critical path, pm-bzmeaa reachability), and emit a completion-date distribution per milestone. Report P50/P85/P95, not a date.\n3. **Reference-class conditioning.** Condition samples on type/tag/parent class so a Chore-heavy remainder is not forecast with Feature-shaped throughput; back off to the global class when a class has too few samples (the same hierarchical shrinkage as pm-4k6b catalog algorithm (21) — small workspaces get wide, honest intervals instead of a special path).\n4. **Scope-growth modeling.** Forecast against a scope that grows: measure the historical rate at which new items enter a milestone's subtree and sample it as a random variable. A forecast that assumes fixed scope is wrong by construction in a living backlog.\n5. **Calibration ledger.** Every published forecast is recorded; when the milestone closes, the realized date is scored against the interval. Publish the hit rate. An uncalibrated forecast is worse than none, and only a recorded track record can prove calibration.\n\n## Delivery\n\nSDK primitives (sampling, simulation, calibration scoring) plus a package surface — extends the existing `pm-brief` reporting package alongside pm-pee5 rather than adding a core command or a new package. Raw simulation rows available as NDJSON for jq pipelines.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\n- No estimate-based critical-chain scheduling: `estimated_minutes` is an input signal, never the forecast basis.\n- No resource/capacity modeling of agents — fleet allocation is pm-3oqjnc.\n- No per-item due-date prediction surfaced as a promise; the unit of forecast is a milestone or subtree."
